      'The placental villi pass through three stages. A primary villus is a finger of cytotrophoblast covered by syncytiotrophoblast. When extraembryonic mesoderm grows into its core, it becomes a secondary villus. When blood cells and small capillaries form within that mesoderm, it becomes a tertiary or definitive villus. These capillaries link up with vessels in the chorionic plate and connecting stalk and then with the embryo’s own circulation.',
